  "body": {
    "id": "thm:selected-gaussian-limit",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Let \\((Q_1,Q_2,A_1,A_2)\\) be the centered Gaussian limit of the pre-period loss and ATT coordinates, and let \\((R_1,R_2)\\) be the centered Gaussian post-period limit, independent of the pre-period limit.  For each \\(s\\in\\mathcal S\\), the fitted-score Bartlett estimator with \\(q_n=\\lfloor\\min(n,m)^{1/5}\\rfloor\\) satisfies \\(\\widehat V_s^{\\mathrm{conv}}\\to_{P_n}V_s\\) and \\(\\Pr_{P_n}\\{\\widehat V_s^{\\mathrm{conv}}>0\\}\\to1\\).  Consequently,\n\\[\n \\sqrt n(\\widehat\\tau_{\\mathrm{sel}}-\\tau)\n \\Rightarrow A_{J(d)}+\\sqrt rR_{J(d)},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n \\frac{\\widehat\\tau_{\\mathrm{sel}}-\\tau}\n {\\widehat{\\operatorname{se}}^{\\mathrm{conv}}_{\\widehat J}}\n \\Rightarrow\n T_1\\mathbf 1\\{X\\leq k\\}+T_2\\mathbf 1\\{X>k\\}.\n\\]\nOn the explicit common stationary square subclass certified by \\(\\texttt{prop:shi-anchor-transfer}\\), fixing a predeclared role, or considering a separate sequence for which \\(|\\sqrt n(L_1-L_2)|\\to\\infty\\), reduces these displays to the corresponding fixed-role centered normal law.  This is a common-subclass reduction, not a set-inclusion claim for the full published class or a claim about its rectangular application procedure.",
    "depends_on": [
      "lem:fixed-role-linearization",
      "lem:conventional-hac-consistency",
      "lem:ridge-loss-expansion",
      "ass:local-loss-gap",
      "ass:sample-ratio",
      "ass:att-variance-margin",
      "ass:selector-variance-margin",
      "def:ridge-selector",
      "def:conventional-selected-att",
      "prop:shi-anchor-transfer"
    ],
    "justification": "The theorem identifies the first-order law reported after outcome-based donor/proxy role choice and isolates the local selected-coordinate nonregularity.",
    "gap": "Leeb and Pötscher provide the generic local post-selection perspective, Rakshit et al. study selection with potentially invalid proxies, and Berk et al. use simultaneous protection. None gives this switched valid-role proximal ATT law; the Shi comparison is restricted to the proved common subclass.",
    "consumer": "An analyst comparing two scientifically credible West-German role assignments can calculate the conventional selected limit while recognizing that the published rectangular application rule is outside the theorem.",
    "proof_tex": "By \\(\\texttt{lem:ridge-loss-expansion}\\), jointly for the two roles,\n\\[\n \\sqrt n\\{(\\widehat L_1-\\widehat L_2)-(L_1-L_2)\\}\n =n^{-1/2}\\sum_{t=1}^n\\{(\\ell_{1,t}-L_1)-(\\ell_{2,t}-L_2)\\}+o_{P_n}(1).\n\\]\nThe stipulated centered Gaussian limit, \\(\\texttt{ass:local-loss-gap}\\), and \\(D=Q_1-Q_2\\) yield\n\\[\n \\sqrt n(\\widehat L_1-\\widehat L_2)\\Rightarrow d+D.\n\\]\nBy \\(\\texttt{ass:selector-variance-margin}\\), \\(\\operatorname{Var}(D)\\geq\\nu>0\\).  Hence \\(d+D\\) has no atom at zero, even if the complete stacked covariance is singular, and the tie rule implies\n\\[\n \\widehat J\\Rightarrow J(d),\\qquad\n J(d)=1\\text{ on }\\{d+D\\leq0\\},\\quad J(d)=2\\text{ otherwise}.\n\\]\nMore explicitly, the probability of the discontinuity strip is bounded by \\(2\\epsilon/\\sqrt{2\\pi\\nu}\\), so continuous upper and lower interpolations followed by \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\) justify this indicator mapping.\n\nFor each role, \\(\\texttt{lem:fixed-role-linearization}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\sqrt n(\\widehat\\tau_s-\\tau)\n =n^{-1/2}\\sum_{t=1}^n\\psi_{s,t}\n +\\sqrt{n/m}\\,m^{-1/2}\\sum_{t=n+1}^{n+m}v_{s,t}+o_{P_n}(1).\n\\]\nThe joint Gaussian premise, independence of the post limit from the pre limit, and \\(n/m\\to r\\) from \\(\\texttt{ass:sample-ratio}\\) therefore imply\n\\[\n \\left(\\sqrt n(\\widehat\\tau_1-\\tau),\\sqrt n(\\widehat\\tau_2-\\tau),\n \\sqrt n(\\widehat L_1-\\widehat L_2)\\right)\n \\Rightarrow(A_1+\\sqrt rR_1,A_2+\\sqrt rR_2,d+D).\n\\]\nThe switching map is discontinuous only when \\(d+D=0\\), a null event.  Thus\n\\[\n \\sqrt n(\\widehat\\tau_{\\mathrm{sel}}-\\tau)\n \\Rightarrow A_{J(d)}+\\sqrt rR_{J(d)}.\n\\]\n\nBy \\(\\texttt{lem:conventional-hac-consistency}\\),\n\\[\n \\max_{s\\in\\mathcal S}|\\widehat V_s^{\\mathrm{conv}}-V_s|\\to_{P_n}0,\n \\qquad\n \\Pr_{P_n}\\{\\min_s\\widehat V_s^{\\mathrm{conv}}\\geq\\nu/2\\}\\to1.\n\\]\nSelection cannot enlarge the maximum error.  On this event the inverse square-root map is Lipschitz with a constant depending only on \\(\\nu\\); tightness of the selected numerator makes replacement of the true inverse standard deviation by the estimated one an \\(o_{P_n}(1)\\) perturbation, and continuous upper and lower approximations therefore give\n\\[\n \\frac{\\widehat\\tau_{\\mathrm{sel}}-\\tau}\n {\\widehat{\\operatorname{se}}^{\\mathrm{conv}}_{\\widehat J}}\n \\Rightarrow\n \\frac{A_1+\\sqrt rR_1}{\\sqrt{V_1}}\\mathbf1\\{d+D\\leq0\\}\n +\\frac{A_2+\\sqrt rR_2}{\\sqrt{V_2}}\\mathbf1\\{d+D>0\\}.\n\\]\nSince \\(X=D/\\sigma_D\\) and \\(k=-d/\\sigma_D\\), this is exactly\n\\[\n T_1\\mathbf1\\{X\\leq k\\}+T_2\\mathbf1\\{X>k\\}.\n\\]\nIf a role is fixed, the indicators disappear and \\(T_s\\sim N(0,1)\\), because \\(V_s=\\operatorname{Var}(A_s+\\sqrt rR_s)>0\\).  On a separate sequence with \\(|\\sqrt n(L_1-L_2)|\\to\\infty\\), the centered loss fluctuation is tight and the population sign selects one role with probability tending to one, giving the same reduction.  Proposition \\(\\texttt{prop:shi-anchor-transfer}\\) places the explicit stationary square family in the intersection with Shi et al.'s fixed-role class.  This proves only the stated common-subclass comparison, not inclusion of either full class in the other and not a claim about the rectangular application rule.",
